  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R3, R4, and R9 already apply simulated tensile deformation across temperatures to the closely related FCC CoNiCrFeMn system and identify partial-dislocation activity. R6, R17, and R18 directly connect low stacking-fault energy, stacking faults, and local FCC/HCP stacking changes in Cantor-type alloys, while R1, R2, and R19 establish high-rate atomistic loading over broad temperature ranges. An expert could therefore propose a bulk single-crystal tensile MD study varying temperature, strain rate, and orientation and expect partial-dislocation/stacking-fault-mediated plasticity. The precise crystallographic-direction comparison, vacancy-cluster nucleation, negligible rate sensitivity of yield strength, and absence of twinning or bulk transformation are not directly supplied, but they are refinements rather than a substantial new intervention concept."
